App 29: The Instrument Library

Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation.

What It Is
A curated, provenance-aware library of scientific instruments, their specifications, their calibration histories, their operational envelopes, and their measurement uncertainties. Each entry describes one instrument (or one instrument class) with the fields a researcher, an engineer, or a data pipeline needs in order to know what the instrument measures, how well it measures it, under what conditions, and with what traceability. The library is a first-class artifact in the suite: other apps query it to autofill instrument metadata, to validate measurement claims, to attach uncertainty budgets, and to reproduce observations.

It reuses the Autofill Library Service (App 49), the STAG Schema Library (App 17), the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20), the PROV-O Exporter (App 3), the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23), and the Revision History Viewer (App 7).

It is for researchers, instrument operators, metrologists, data engineers, and the users of every app in the suite that records, cites, or reasons about measurements.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Store instrument entries with a stable schema.

Each entry describes one instrument or one instrument class.

Support instrument identification: name, manufacturer, model, serial number, asset tag.

Support instrument classification: type, modality, measurement domain, physical principle.

Support specifications: range, resolution, accuracy, precision, sensitivity, linearity, drift, bandwidth, sampling rate.

Support operational envelope: temperature, pressure, humidity, voltage, magnetic field, radiation, vibration.

Support calibration records: date, standard used, traceability chain, calibration certificate, uncertainty.

Support measurement uncertainty: a full uncertainty budget per measurement mode.

Support traceability: a chain to a national metrology institute or a primary standard.

Support provenance: depositor, date, source, tool version, references.

Support versioning: each entry has a version, and each edit creates a new version.

Support curation: entries are submitted as pending, then approved, rejected, or edited by a moderator.

Support search: by name, by type, by domain, by specification range, by uncertainty, by traceability.

Support comparison: compare two or more instruments field by field.

Support export: JSON, JSON Lines, CSV, Turtle, PROV-O.

Support import: bulk import from JSON or JSON Lines.

Support autofill: the Autofill Library Service (App 49) reads this library under library_id instrument_library.

Support integration with App 23 (Physics Knowledge Library) so that instrument entries can be cited by physics claims.

Support integration with App 20 (Provenance-Aware Annotation Database) so that instrument annotations carry provenance.

Support integration with App 7 (Revision History Viewer) so that every edit is visible.

Support a REST API.

Support a GraphQL API.

Support a command-line interface.

Support a web interface for browsing, submitting, comparing, and moderating.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same query and same library produce the same result.

Fast: a lookup returns in under 100 milliseconds for a library of up to 100,000 entries.

Scalable: support up to 10^7 instrument entries.

Versioned: the library version, the entry version, and the service version are recorded.

Reproducible: every entry has a complete provenance record.

Portable: Linux, macOS, Windows.

Well-documented: API documentation, schema documentation, user guide, metrology primer.

